n best support cities like  l.a  well you know i remember what it was  like when joe biden was in the white  house because it was just 2013 i was a  new mayor i was invited  um with about a dozen mayors from around  the country who had just been elected  from detroit and la and chattanooga and  new york and other places and i was  expecting to go there for a photo  op and we instead in the roosevelt room  had a two-hour private conversation  where they just listened to every city  and every city's incoming mayor talk  about america  i think first and foremost rather than  programs or people  it is that perspective that i'm just  looking forward to because once you  develop that relationship  and that listening um you don't know  when you'll need them you don't know  what you'll need them for you don't know  the great idea that you want to  take up to the national level for them  but establishing that relationship  and joe biden really is who you see he  has that heart he's a great talker and a  great listener  um and and knows what he's talking about  so i think  that's the way this first starts helping  cities specifically though i would set  up something like a local government  council  in the white house that would bring  together city county and some state  leaders to be a regular group to bounce  things off of and to ask for them to  distribute kind of  initiatives and then i would have an  urban agenda that starts really with  opportunity in our cities  um what what businesses closed down uh  what people did not get ppp  loans uh what workers disproportionately  lost their jobs in the latino community  for instance and a lot of people in  hospitality and frontline jobs  really have been decimated and and help  um  mayors and cities be the eyes and ears  of an american recovery  that's what i would wish thanks neha  next we have a question from brad austin  hey brad i'm eric garcetti uh  my name is brad austin and i'm a first  year mba student here at stanford